## Why This Is Asked

Tests per-tenant Saga resilience, isolation, and recovery with Event Sourcing and outbox.

## Key Concepts

- CQRS and Event Sourcing
- Saga orchestration and compensations
- Per-tenant isolation and outbox pattern
- Idempotent event handlers and replay

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal orchestrator sketch (pseudo)
function startSaga(tenantId, jobId){
  dispatchEvent({tenantId, jobId, type:'IngestRequested'});
}
function onEvent(event){
  if(event.type==='IngestSucceeded') emit('TranscodeRequested', event);
  if(event.type==='IngestFailed' || event.type==='TranscodeFailed' || event.type==='WatermarkFailed'){
    emit('Compensate', {tenantId:event.tenantId, jobId:event.jobId, steps:['Ingest','Transcode','Watermark']});
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you ensure exactly-once processing for the tenant outbox?
- How would you test failure injection and crash recovery in this Saga?

## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates your ability to design distributed workflows across microservices with CQRS/ES, ensuring tenant isolation, idempotency, and reliable message delivery.

## Key Concepts
- Long-running sagas with compensating transactions for rollback scenarios
- CQRS and Event Sourcing with versioned events for auditability and replay
- Outbox pattern ensuring at-least-once delivery
- Idempotency via correlationId and deduplication checks
- Per-tenant read models maintaining data isolation

## Code Example
```javascript
// Event-to-command mapping with deduplication
function handleEvent(evt, state) {
  if (state.processed?.includes(evt.id)) return;

  const cmd = mapEventToCommand(evt);
  enqueueToOutbox(cmd);
  state.processed = [...(state.processed || []), evt.id];
}

// Saga orchestration with compensation
async function executeOnboardSaga(tenantId, correlationId) {
  try {
    await TenantAuth.create(tenantId, correlationId);
    await Billing.setupQuota(tenantId, correlationId);
    await DataIngestion.provision(tenantId, correlationId);
    await Analytics.initialize(tenantId, correlationId);
  } catch (error) {
    await compensateSaga(tenantId, correlationId);
  }
}

async function compensateSaga(tenantId, correlationId) {
  await Analytics.deprovision(tenantId, correlationId);
  await DataIngestion.stop(tenantId, correlationId);
  await Billing.revertQuota(tenantId, correlationId);
  await TenantAuth.delete(tenantId, correlationId);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design practical cache invalidation and explain trade-offs of Redis vs Memcached in a beginner context.

## Key Concepts

- cache-aside pattern
- tenant isolation in keys
- stampede mitigation with locks
- event-bus invalidation

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudocode illustrating lazy reload with lock
async function getProduct(tid, pid) {
  const key = `tenant:${tid}:product:${pid}`
  let val = cache.get(key)
  if (val) return val
  const lock = `lock:${tid}:${pid}`
  if (redis.set(lock, 1, 'NX', 'PX', 1000)) {
    val = db.fetchProduct(tid, pid)
    cache.set(key, val, 300)
    redis.del(lock)
  } else {
    await sleep(50)
    return getProduct(tid, pid)
  }
  return val
}
```

