## Why This Is Asked
Tests advanced distributed systems concepts: sagas, event sourcing, CQRS, and failure handling at scale.

## Key Concepts
- Saga orchestration vs choreography trade-offs
- Event sourcing with immutable event streams
- Idempotency via correlation IDs and deduplication
- Compensating actions for distributed transactions
- CQRS separation for read/write optimization

## Code Example
```javascript
// Saga orchestrator pseudo-code
class OrderSaga {
  async handleOrderCreated(event) {
    const { orderId, items, total } = event.data;

    const inventoryResult = await this.reserveInventory(orderId, items);
    if (!inventoryResult.success) {
      await this.compensateWithOrderCancelled(orderId);
      return;
    }

    const paymentResult = await this.authorizePayment(orderId, total);
    if (!paymentResult.success) {
      await this.releaseInventory(orderId, items);
      await this.compensateWithOrderCancelled(orderId);
      return;
    }

    await this.createShipping(orderId, event.data.shippingAddress);
    await this.completeOrder(orderId);
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test end-to-end saga correctness across services?
- What tracing and metrics would you collect for correlation across microservices?
- How would you handle saga timeout scenarios and cleanup?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design cross-protocol consistency, idempotency, and contract-driven evolution at scale across REST, GraphQL, and gRPC.

## Key Concepts

- Unified, idempotent write path across REST/GraphQL/gRPC
- Centralized idempotency store (e.g., Redis) keyed by client+request_id
- Event-sourced write model with an outbox for eventual consistency
- Per-protocol adapters and a single source of truth OpenAPI/SDL/proto
- Observability: tracing, rate limiting, and safe schema evolution

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo: idempotent write handler sketch
async function createReview(req, res) {
  const idKey = req.headers['Idempotency-Key'] || req.body.idempotencyKey;
  const key = `idemp:reviews:${idKey}`;
  const cached = await redis.get(key);
  if (cached) return res.status(200).json(JSON.parse(cached));

  // validate and emit event
  const event = { id: generateId(), type: 'ReviewCreated', payload: req.body };
  eventBus.publish(event);

  const created = { id: event.id, ...req.body };
  await redis.set(key, JSON.stringify(created), 'EX', 3600);
  res.status(201).json(created);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test idempotency across REST/GraphQL/gRPC?
- How do you handle conflicting content with the same idempotencyKey?

